  "narrative": "The metabolomics data reveal a striking enrichment in **tryptophan metabolism** (KEGG map00380) as the dominant pathway, with a fold-enrichment of 545.87 and FDR effectively zero across six input metabolites: L-tryptophan, serotonin, melatonin, indoleacetaldehyde, tryptamine, and quinolinic acid. This strongly positions the aromatic amino acid L-tryptophan at the centre of the affected biology.\n\nThe KEGG reaction graph confirms directional links. **L-tryptophan (C00078) is directly converted to tryptamine (C00398)** in a single enzymatic step, consistent with the co-occurrence of both metabolites. **Serotonin (C00780) is upstream of melatonin (C01598)**, traversing N-acetylserotonin as an intermediate in two enzymatic steps, a well-established biosynthetic sequence in pineal and neural tissues. Tryptophan also feeds into the indole branch, yielding indoleacetaldehyde (C00637) and subsequently indole (C00463). Notably, **quinolinic acid (C03722) could not be connected to L-tryptophan within six KEGG reaction hops** in this query — this likely reflects its origin in the separate kynurenine arm of tryptophan catabolism rather than the serotonin/melatonin branch.\n\nThe biological significance is considerable. Tryptophan is the precursor for three critical axes: the serotonergic pathway (tryptamine → serotonin), the melatonergic pathway (serotonin → N-acetylserotonin → melatonin), and the kynurenine/quinolinic acid axis linked to neuroinflammation and NMDA-receptor activation. Simultaneous elevation of metabolites across these branches in a treatment-versus-control design suggests a broad remodelling of tryptophan partitioning — potentially reflecting immune activation (quinolinic acid), circadian or mood-relevant neurochemical shifts (serotonin/melatonin), and gut-microbiome–derived indole signalling (indole, indoleacetaldehyde). The presence of pyrophosphate, succinate, iron, and glucose alongside these indole derivatives hints at broader energetic and mitochondrial cofactor co-disruption consistent with inflammatory or oxidative-stress conditions.",
